## **1. Security Testing – Highest Priority**

Critical

These tests protect the application against hackers, unauthorized access, data theft and cyberattacks.

| No. | Security test              | What must be verified                                                           |
| --- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 1   | SQL Injection              | Malicious SQL cannot access or manipulate database records.                     |
| 2   | Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) | Malicious scripts cannot execute through inputs or displayed content.           |
| 3   | CSRF Protection            | Unauthorized requests cannot be submitted using another user's session.         |
| 4   | Broken Authentication      | Login mechanisms cannot be bypassed.                                            |
| 5   | Password Security          | Passwords are securely hashed, never stored in plain text.                      |
| 6   | Brute Force Protection     | Repeated login attempts trigger rate limits or temporary blocking.              |
| 7   | Session Hijacking          | Stolen or manipulated sessions cannot be used to access accounts.               |
| 8   | Session Expiry             | Sessions expire appropriately after inactivity or logout.                       |
| 9   | JWT Security               | Tokens have appropriate expiry, signing and validation.                         |
| 10  | Privilege Escalation       | Teachers cannot gain admin permissions.                                         |
| 11  | File Upload Security       | Malicious files cannot be uploaded and executed.                                |
| 12  | API Security               | APIs reject unauthorized and malformed requests.                                |
| 13  | Sensitive Data Exposure    | Passwords, tokens and private student information are not exposed.              |
| 14  | HTTPS Enforcement          | All production traffic uses HTTPS.                                              |
| 15  | Security Headers           | CSP, HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options and other appropriate headers are configured. |
| 16  | Directory Traversal        | Users cannot access restricted server files.                                    |
| 17  | Remote Code Execution      | User inputs cannot execute arbitrary server-side code.                          |
| 18  | Open Redirect              | Attackers cannot redirect users to malicious websites through trusted links.    |
| 19  | CORS Security              | Only authorized origins can access protected APIs.                              |
| 20  | Dependency Vulnerabilities | Third-party libraries do not contain known exploitable vulnerabilities.         |

**Important:** Use the OWASP Top 10 and OWASP ASVS as security testing references. For mobile apps, also follow OWASP MASVS.

References:

- [OWASP Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
- [OWASP ASVS](https://owasp.org/www-project-application-security-verification-standard/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
- [OWASP Mobile Application Security](https://mas.owasp.org/MASVS/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

## **2. Multi-School Data Isolation Testing**

Most critical for Rai Plus

Your platform will serve multiple schools. This is one of the most important testing categories.

Imagine:

- School A: Virat International School
- School B: Karan Public School
- School C: Another customer school

All three schools must operate independently.

| No. | Test                                        | Expected result                          |
| --- | ------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| 1   | School A accessing School B student records | Access denied                            |
| 2   | School B accessing School A fee records     | Access denied                            |
| 3   | Changing school ID in API requests          | Access denied                            |
| 4   | Manipulating URL parameters                 | No cross-school data exposure            |
| 5   | Changing tenant ID in browser storage       | No unauthorized access                   |
| 6   | Accessing another school's documents        | Access denied                            |
| 7   | Searching students globally                 | Only authorized school results           |
| 8   | Downloading another school's reports        | Access denied                            |
| 9   | Accessing another school's dashboard        | Access denied                            |
| 10  | Background jobs and scheduled tasks         | Process data within the correct school   |
| 11  | School-specific settings                    | No cross-school modification             |
| 12  | School-specific notifications               | No cross-school notification leakage     |
| 13  | Cache isolation                             | Cached information is tenant-specific    |
| 14  | Exporting Excel/CSV                         | Only authorized tenant data exported     |
| 15  | Database queries                            | Tenant restrictions enforced server-side |

**Mandatory scenario:** Log in as a teacher from School A, manually modify API requests, student IDs and school IDs, and attempt to retrieve School B's records.

The test must fail at the server/API level, not merely hide the information on the screen.

